Issues.Body:
### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

common

### Is this a regression?

No

### Description

When `HttpTransferCache` is active during SSR (enabled by default via `provideClientHydration()`), fetching a `Blob` via `HttpClient.get(..., {responseType: 'blob'})` or `httpResource.blob(...)` serializes an empty string (`""`) into `TransferState`. During client hydration, reading that cached entry returns a 0-byte `Blob` with `type === ''` instead of the server response body.

This happens because [`transferCacheInterceptorFn`](https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/0dafb368349f6d2c436296801f2f642747899d2d/packages/common/http/src/transfer_cache.ts#L329-L337) passes `Blob` bodies straight to [`toBase64`](https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/0dafb368349f6d2c436296801f2f642747899d2d/packages/common/http/src/transfer_cache.ts#L436-L450), which calls `new Uint8Array(buffer as ArrayBufferLike)`. Because a `Blob` is not an `ArrayBufferLike`, `new Uint8Array(blob)` evaluates to a 0-length `Uint8Array` and `toBase64` returns `""`. On the client, [`retrieveStateFromCache`](https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/0dafb368349f6d2c436296801f2f642747899d2d/packages/common/http/src/transfer_cache.ts#L253-L255) then reconstructs `new Blob([fromBase64("")])`.

The existing `'should cache blob responses correctly'` spec in [`transfer_cache_spec.ts`](https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/0dafb368349f6d2c436296801f2f642747899d2d/packages/common/http/test/transfer_cache_spec.ts#L573-L583) missed this because it only checks the initial `HttpTestingController` response and never inspects `TransferState` or makes a follow-up request to read from the cache (unlike the `arraybuffer` spec right above it).

Minimal reproduction (in `packages/common/http/test/transfer_cache_spec.ts` with `withHttpTransferCache({})`):

```ts
const testBlob = new Blob([new Uint8Array([10, 20, 30, 40, 50])], {
  type: 'application/octet-stream',
});

// 1. Server request
globalThis['ngServerMode'] = true;
TestBed.inject(HttpClient).get('/test-blob', {responseType: 'blob'}).subscribe();
TestBed.inject(HttpTestingController).expectOne('/test-blob').flush(testBlob);
// TransferState now stores { b: "" }

// 2. Client hydration reads from TransferState
globalThis['ngServerMode'] = false;
let cachedResponse!: Blob;
TestBed.inject(HttpClient)
  .get('/test-blob', {responseType: 'blob'})
  .subscribe((res) => (cachedResponse = res));
TestBed.inject(HttpTestingController).expectNone('/test-blob');

// Actual:   cachedResponse.size === 0, cachedResponse.type === ''
// Expected: cachedResponse.size === 5, cachedResponse.type === 'application/octet-stream'
```

### Please provide a link to a minimal reproduction of the bug

_No response_

### Please provide the exception or error you saw

No exception is thrown; `TransferState` silently stores `b: ""` and the client receives an empty 0-byte `Blob`.

### Please provide the environment you discovered this bug in (run `ng version`)

```text
Angular: 22.3.0-next.4 (main @ 0dafb36834)
Node.js: v26.10.0
OS: macOS arm64 (Darwin 25.5.0)
```

### Anything else?

Related to #66827 and PR #66835, which added base64 encoding for `arraybuffer` and `blob` responses in `HttpTransferCache`.
